WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.952
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.065 --> 00:00:06.110
My wife likes to tell people
that he looks very nice.

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:07.020
He's a very nice guy.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:08.330
He's a very calm guy.

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:10.592
But, he has a dark side to him.

00:00:10.592 --> 00:00:12.018
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.018 --> 00:00:13.810
As a thriller writer,
my job is to look out

00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:15.610
into the real world,
see the problem side.

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:16.710
I create the problems.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:18.978
Then during the course of
the novel, I solve them.

00:00:18.978 --> 00:00:20.770
You don't want the
reader to sort of settle

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:22.150
back and be really
comfortable and, like,

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:23.530
drifting through these pages.

00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:25.863
You want to grab them by the
throat right from the get-go

00:00:25.863 --> 00:00:27.955
and hurdle them through
the next 450 pages.

00:00:27.955 --> 00:00:29.780
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:32.740
I wanted to get into the
weeds with all of you

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:34.660
about how I go about my craft.

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:35.600
Great characters.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:36.808
How do you come up with them?

00:00:36.808 --> 00:00:38.920
How do you pace a
story so that people

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:40.840
can't stop turning the pages?

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:43.630
All the things that go into
making a successful writing

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:46.500
career, I've tried to deliver
to you during this MasterClass.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.130
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:50.930
I was a lawyer when I first
started writing my first novel.

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:52.420
I would spend time
with my family.

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:53.320
They would go off to bed.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.060
And around 10 o'clock
I would go downstairs

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:56.810
and I would write till
two in the morning.

00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:59.510
Every day, seven days a week,
and I did that for years.

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:01.870
If you want to be
creative writing stories,

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:03.972
you have to be very
creative in finding the time

00:01:03.972 --> 00:01:04.930
to write those stories.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:05.890
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:07.640
Research, for me, is critical.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:10.638
This binder is my nuke binder.

00:01:10.638 --> 00:01:12.180
And the section I'm
gonna read to you

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:15.010
is about two and a half pages
long. Two and a half pages

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:16.120
versus this.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:16.820
Do this.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:18.870
Leave almost all of it out.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:19.870
You will thank me later.

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:23.670
I had insecurities.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:25.120
You will have insecurities.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.360
So any writer out there
who's doubting their ability

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.680
to actually sit down and tell
a story, I'm telling you,

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:31.440
I got your back on this.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:32.670
Writers change the world.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.230
Books change the world.

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:36.540
As much as you might
need a class from me,

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:38.070
I, and the rest
of the publishing

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:41.220
world and the world in general,
we need you a lot more.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:47.600
I'm David Baldacci, and
this is my MasterClass.

